{"id":"pulp-fantasy","title":"Pulp Fantasy","url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Pulp_Fantasy","rev":239261,"fetched":"2026-10-16","lead":[{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Pulp Fantasy"]}," is a design aesthetic that was prevalent from 1960s to 1970s that features muscular heroism, raw, exotic, kitschy landscapes, and a retro-sensationalism as a distinct element. It was rooted in the cheaply, airbrushed, printed \"pulp\" magazines and later heavily popularized by fantasy/sci-fi media, paperback art and progressive rock album covers. Key elements of the aesthetic includes dramatic chiaroscuro lighting, intense action, and an untamed magic."]}],"sections":[{"title":"Visuals","id":"visuals","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["The aesthetic focuses heavily on surreal, moody environments that often feature blood-red twin moons, looming monolithic citadels, untamed primordial jungles, and eerie glowing caverns. It was often dominated by distressed, bold typography, high-stakes action scenes, incorporating hyper-muscular figures, visceral motion, smoke, and atmospheric backgrounds."]}]},{"title":"Media","id":"media","blocks":[{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Film"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Barbarella: Queen of the Galaxy (1968)"]},{"c":["The Land That Time Forgot (1974)"]},{"c":["Conan the Barbarian (1982)"]},{"c":["Fire and Ice (1983)"]},{"c":["Big Trouble in Little China (1986)"]},{"c":["Masters of the Universe (1987)"]},{"c":["The Shadow (1994)"]},{"c":["The Phantom (1996)"]},{"c":["Sky Captain and the World of Tomorrow (2004)"]},{"c":["The Warlords (2007)"]},{"c":["Solomon Kane (2009)"]},{"c":["John Carter (2012)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Video Games"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Golden Axe series (1989-)"]}]}]},{"title":"Gallery","id":"gallery","blocks":[{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/7/7c/Starcastle.webp/revision/latest?cb=20260223164135","w":185,"h":185},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/3/3e/Showtime.webp/re